## Part 1 — `calc.A()` returns the unit value

**File:** `services/calc/calc.go`
**Serves:** `/go-test/go-test:fixture.buildcodefix.tests.suite::TestA`

**Contract.** `A()` is an exported, zero-argument function on package
`calc` returning `int`. Its specified value is `1`. It is deterministic
and side-effect free: every call returns the same value, and repeated
calls under `-count=1` or any higher count must not diverge.

**Intended implementation.** Replace the placeholder `return 0` in the
body of `A` with the specified constant `1`. Keep the signature exactly
`func A() int` — the suite calls it with no arguments and compares the
result directly to an untyped integer constant, so neither the parameter
list nor the return type may change. Keep the function exported
(capital `A`): the test is in `package suite_test` in a different package
and reaches it through the import path.

After this part, `TestA`'s guard `calc.A()!=1` is false and the
`t.Fatal("A needs one")` branch is no longer reached.

## Part 2 — `calc.B()` returns two

**File:** `services/calc/calc.go`
**Serves:** `/go-test/go-test:fixture.buildcodefix.tests.suite::TestB`

**Contract.** `B()` is an exported, zero-argument function on package
